{"type":"message","id":"4c6bc261","parentId":"a3c1e8bf","timestamp":"2026-05-21T21:45:40.966Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The heartbeat messages are all from the automated heartbeat system itself. The latest messages in both threads end around 23:50 UTC (timestamp 1778975520833). The current time is 21:45 UTC, which seems to be earlier than the latest messages shown (23:50 UTC). Wait, actually the timestamps are Unix timestamps in milliseconds.\n\nLet me check: 1778975520833 ms = 1778975520.833 seconds from Unix epoch.\nCurrent time: 2026-05-21 21:45 UTC = Unix timestamp around 1783009500 (2026 is about 6 years after 2020, so roughly 6*365.25*24*3600 ≈ 189,216,000 seconds after 2020... let me think: Unix timestamp for 2026-05-21 21:45 UTC would be around 177... actually wait.\n\nLet me recalculate: Unix timestamp for 2026-01-01 00:00 UTC:\n- From 1970 to 2026 = 56 years\n- 56 * 365.25 * 24 * 3600 ≈ 1,767,238,400 seconds\n- Plus days to May 21 = 140 days * 86400 = 12,096,000\n- Plus 21:45 = 78300 seconds\n- Total: ≈ 1,779,412,700\n\nWait, the timestamps shown are around 1,778,974,xxx which is in May 2026. Current time is 21:45 UTC, and the latest heartbeat is 23:50 UTC - that's about 2 hours in the future? That doesn't make sense. Let me check if maybe the timestamps shown are actually 23:50 UTC on a different date, or the cron time is offset.\n\nActually, looking at the heartbeat messages, they mention UTC times in the content like \"23:50 UTC\". \n\nThe timestamps in the messages seem to correspond to those reported times. The latest heartbeat around 23:50 UTC comes from May 21st based on the current time. Looking at the message content and timestamps, the most recent entries show 23:50 UTC heartbeats from the same day. Since the current time is 21:45 UTC, those 23:50 UTC messages would be about 2 hours ahead, which suggests there might be some time discrepancy I need to reconcile.\n\nI'm not finding any human-generated 